{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792580628765,"sessionID":"ses_ea1de83daffetTStmPb5FvGcdC","part":{"partID":"prt_15e21b0530027Z9MokqWcqJXWL","sessionID":"ses_ea1de83daffetTStmPb5FvGcdC","messageID":"msg_15e21a691001AomlTBL2pTgu0A","type":"tool","id":"call_97aa972327b04778912b30e7","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/milin/2026/软件开发/application-atlas/docs/template-factory/go-minimax-batch-2026-10-21/personal-kanban/calls/blueprint-spec-review-aux/leaf.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/milin/2026/软件开发/application-atlas/docs/template-factory/go-minimax-batch-2026-10-21/personal-kanban/calls/blueprint-spec-review-aux/leaf.md, lines 1-213\n1: # Kanban Task Board\n2: \n3: ## Overview\n4: \n5: A **Kanban Task Board** is a work-management application whose world is a visual board: work is represented as cards, cards are held in ordered columns, and a card's position on the board is the record of where that work stands. Moving a card from column to column is how progress happens and how progress is seen.\n6: \n7: The defining core is small:\n8: \n9: ```text\n10: Board (the organizing surface: ordered columns)\n11: └── Card (the unit of work)\n12:     └── Position-as-state (column membership = the card's status;\n13:         moving the card = the state change; column order = progression\n14:         toward done; card order = sequence/priority)\n15: ```\n16: \n17: Everything else the market associates with the category — card details, team collaboration, WIP limits, swimlanes, templates, automation, analytics, extra views — is standard or optional structure layered on that core, not what defines it. The software's ancestry is a physical whiteboard or wall with card notes; the product persists, shares, and scales that grammar. When the primary working model shifts away from the board — to a flat checklist, to task records with status fields viewed in lists, or to an ordered backlog driving timeboxed delivery — the product has crossed into a neighboring Application Type.\n18: \n19: ## Users & Context\n20: \n21: Two user populations share one structure:\n22: \n23: - **Individuals** use a board as a personal work surface: a private to-do flow (to-do → doing → done), a plan being assembled, a small project. A single-user board is a first-class form, not a degenerate case.\n24: - **Team members** work from a shared board that mirrors the team's own process. Members move cards as they take up and finish work; the board is the team's shared picture of who is doing what.\n25: - **Stakeholders and managers** largely read the board rather than operate it: status at a glance, where work is piling up, what is blocked or aging.\n26: \n27: The typical context is an ongoing flow of work rather than a fixed plan: requests and tasks arrive, get taken up, move through a few recognizable stages, and complete. Boards serve many settings — product and engineering teams, marketing and operations, sales processes, personal productivity — because the columns are defined by the users, not by the product.\n28: \n29: ## Core Model\n30: \n31: ### The Defining Core\n32: \n33: **The board.** The board is the primary working surface and the model of record — not one view over some other task database. A board usually frames one process, team, project, or purpose. It is divided into vertical **columns**, arranged left to right in the order the users' process unfolds.\n34: \n35: **The column (list / stage).** A named container for cards at the same point of the process. Column names come from the users' own workflow — backlog-ish, in-progress-ish, review-ish, done-ish — and are freely configurable: renamed, added, reordered, split. Column membership is meaningful: it is where the work stands. Columns may be subdivided into sub-columns for finer steps, and may carry capacity rules (see WIP limits under standard capabilities). Horizontal rows — **swimlanes** — can cross-cut the columns to separate types of work or classes of service.\n36: \n37: **The card.** A self-contained unit of work: a task, request, deliverable, or item to handle. A card carries its own details on its face and inside — title, description, assignee or members, due date, labels or colors, attachments, checklists, comments, and a history of what happened to it. Cards are movable; that movability is the point.\n38: \n39: **Position as state.** The card's column membership is the status. There is no separate status field that must be kept in sync: to change the state of the work, you move the card. Column order expresses progression from not-started toward done; the order of cards within a column expresses sequence or priority. A completed card either rests in a done column, gets marked complete, or is moved to an archive — the board's terminal handling keeps finished work out of the way without destroying it.\n40: \n41: ### Concept and Implementation\n42: \n43: The core is written conceptually; products realize it with different vocabularies:\n44: \n45: ```text\n46: Concept:   Board        → implementations:  board, project board, workflow board\n47: Concept:   Column       → implementations:  list, lane stage, workflow state column,\n48:                                             sub-columns for finer steps\n49: Concept:   Card         → implementations:  card, task card, work item card\n50: Concept:   Position     → implementations:  column membership (+ optional completion\n51:             as state                       flag, archive move, in-column ordering)\n52: ```\n53: \n54: A reader who has only seen one product's vocabulary should still recognize any other board tool from this model.\n55: \n56: ### Standard Capabilities\n57: \n58: Mature products almost universally add:\n59: \n60: - **Card detail** — description, assignee/members, due dates, labels or colors, attachments, checklists/subtasks, comments with notifications, per-card activity history.\n61: - **Real-time collaboration** — several people working the same board with live updates.\n62: - **Board membership and permissions** — who can see or edit a board; role tiers on larger accounts; per-board access levels.\n63: - **Workflow customization** — rename/add/reorder columns, sub-columns, collapsible columns and swimlanes, board backgrounds.\n64: - **Search and filtering** — find cards across the board (and the archive) by text, member, label, date.\n65: - **Board templates and cloning** — start from a prebuilt flow (task tracking, sales pipeline, product development) or duplicate an existing board.\n66: - **Automation** — rules that move, label, or otherwise act on cards when triggers occur (e.g., \"when a card moves to review, assign a reviewer\").\n67: - **Archiving** — completed cards leave the board but remain searchable.\n68: - **Notifications** — assignments, mentions, and due-date reminders, typically via email or in-app.\n69: - **API, integrations, and mobile clients.**\n70: \n71: ### Optional / Segment-dependent Capabilities\n72: \n73: - **WIP limits** — a cap per column on how many cards it may hold, so the team finishes started work before pulling more. Method-standard for kanban practice and prominent in methodology-first products; not present natively in every general-purpose tool.\n74: - **Swimlanes** — present in many team-oriented products, absent from the simplest forms.\n75: - **Flow analytics** — cumulative flow diagrams, cycle/lead time, aging, forecasting from historical data. The signature of methodology-first and enterprise offerings.\n76: - **Time tracking** — timers and reports attached to cards (a differentiator of some products rather than the norm).\n77: - **Secondary views** — calendar, timeline, table, or dashboard renderings of the same cards.\n78: - **Cross-board machinery** — moving cards between boards, linked board networks, portfolio layers above boards in enterprise suites.\n79: - **AI assistance** — text drafting, board construction from a description, or agents that execute cards on the board (current-generation layer).\n80: \n81: ## How It Works\n82: \n83: ### Shape the board\n84: \n85: ```text\n86: Create a board\n87: → name the columns after the real process (often starting from a template:\n88:    \"to do / doing / done\", or a richer request → in progress → review → done)\n89: → optionally split columns, add swimlanes, set WIP limits\n90: → invite members, set who can view/edit\n91: ```\n92: \n93: The board is configured, not programmed. As the process changes, the columns change — the same vendor guidance everywhere: the board should mirror how the team actually works.\n94: \n95: ### Fill and work the flow\n96: \n97: ```text\n98: Cards enter the board (added by hand, captured from requests/integrations,\n99:    or imported in bulk)\n100: → a member picks up a card (usually from the top of the first column)\n101: → moves it into the working column\n102: → does the work; discusses and attaches material on the card\n103: → moves it onward through review/delivery stages\n104: → it reaches done and is archived or left to rest\n105: ```\n106: \n107: This is a pull loop: people take up work as they free themselves, ideally finishing what they have before starting more. Where WIP limits are configured, the overfilled column becomes visible as a stop signal; how strictly the software enforces the cap varies by product. Dragging the card is not decoration — it *is* the status update, performed by the person doing the work, which is why the board can stay current without a reporting ceremony.\n108: \n109: ### Steer by looking\n110: \n111: The board is designed to be read at a distance: a glance shows what is on deck, what is in motion, what is done, and where cards accumulate. Teams use it in their regular check-ins; bottlenecks show up as fat columns, stalled or blocked work as aging cards, and overload as columns pressed against their limits. Managers and stakeholders read the same surface instead of asking for status.\n112: \n113: ### Adjust over time\n114: \n115: Boards are living structures: columns get renamed, merged, or sliced as the process is refined; cards get moved between boards; finished boards are archived. The improvement loop — look at flow, change the board, work the new flow — is part of the product's normal operation.\n116: \n117: ### Defining core vs standard vs optional\n118: \n119: - **Defining core** — board, cards, position-as-state, movement as the state change.\n120: - **Standard capabilities** — card detail, real-time collaboration, permissions, workflow customization, search/filter, templates, automation, archive, notifications, API/mobile.\n121: - **Optional / variant** — WIP limits, swimlanes, flow analytics, time tracking, secondary views, cross-board linking, AI, deployment choices, personal vs team orientation.\n122: \n123: ## Interfaces\n124: \n125: ### The board surface\n126: \n127: The product's main screen.\n128: \n129: - shows the board: columns left to right, swimlanes across, cards stacked in each column\n130: - cards display a summary face (title, colors/labels, members, due date, checklist progress)\n131: - primary actions: add a card, drag a card within or across columns, filter/search, open a card, collapse areas, open the board menu\n132: \n133: ### Card detail view\n134: \n135: Opens from any card.\n136: \n137: - description, checklist with progress, members, dates, labels, attachments, comments, activity history\n138: - primary actions: edit details, assign, comment, attach, move (or drag from here), copy/move to another board, archive or delete\n139: \n140: ### Board menu / settings\n141: \n142: The board's control panel.\n143: \n144: - members and permissions, background and appearance, automation rules, power-ups/extensions, activity feed, archive access\n145: - primary actions: manage access, configure the workflow (columns, swimlanes, WIP limits), set up automations, search the board\n146: \n147: ### Boards home / switcher\n148: \n149: The entry surface listing a user's boards and available templates.\n150: \n151: - board previews, favorites, team/workspace groupings, template gallery\n152: - primary actions: create a board, start from a template, open a recent board\n153: \n154: ### Secondary surfaces (where offered)\n155: \n156: Calendar, timeline, table, or dashboard renderings of the same cards; analytics screens (cumulative flow, cycle time, aging) in methodology-oriented products.\n157: \n158: ## Important Rules / Behaviors\n159: \n160: - **Position is the status.** The card's column membership is the authoritative state of the work. There is no second status field to reconcile; the board cannot lie about where work stands unless someone fails to move a card.\n161: - **The workflow is user-defined.** The product fixes no particular set of stages. Column names, count, and meaning are the users' own; two boards in the same product can model entirely different processes.\n162: - **Movement is the update operation.** Dragging a card between columns — with the position confirmed in the card's history — is how state changes are made; some products let rules automate specific moves.\n163: - **Capacity rules bind when configured.** A WIP limit makes an overfull column visually (and sometimes mechanically) resist new cards. The pull discipline — finish before starting more — is a practice the board supports, not one software generally enforces.\n164: - **Card order carries sequence.** Within a column, top-to-bottom order serves as the working priority, and members commonly take up cards from the top.\n165: - **Terminal work is preserved, not erased.** Completion moves cards to a done state or an archive; history remains inspectable, and the archive is searchable.\n166: - **Access is scoped per board.** A board has its own member/permission scope; boards can be private, team-shared, or broadly visible depending on the product's model.\n167: - **Boards are cheap to create and disposable by design.** One process or purpose per board; proliferation is expected and handled with a home/switcher surface.\n168: \n169: ## Variants\n170: \n171: - **Personal kanban boards** — single-user boards for private to-dos and planning; the simplest full form of the Type.\n172: - **Team process boards** — shared boards with permissions, swimlanes, and automation; the center of gravity of the market.\n173: - **General-purpose board-first tools** — the archetype: any workflow, any purpose, boards as the whole product.\n174: - **Methodology-first products** — lean/kanban-practice tools that foreground WIP limits, explicit policies, and flow metrics.\n175: - **Boards as a module** — board functionality embedded in task-management, workspace, or project-portfolio suites; the board's own model is unchanged by the packaging.\n176: - **Enterprise deployments** — linked board networks, portfolio layers above boards, enterprise access governance.\n177: - **Deployment forms** — cloud service, self-hosted commercial, open-source self-hosted.\n178: - **Secondary board uses** — idea boards, information and resource hubs: the same surface applied where the \"flow\" degenerates to loose categories.\n179: \n180: ## Related Application Types\n181: \n182: | Application Type | Distinction |\n183: |---|---|\n184: | To-do List Application | centers on a flat checklist with completion ticks and reminders; no board grammar — remove the columns and position-as-state and this Type collapses into it |\n185: | Task Management Application | centers on task *records* with status fields, priority, and list/calendar views; a kanban board there is one projection view over the records, whereas here the board is the native model and position is the state of record — the sharpest seam in this cluster |\n186: | Agile Project Management Application | the board is one surface of that Type; its defining structure adds a team-owned ordered backlog, a bounded delivery cadence (timeboxed iteration or continuous pull) as the re-planning boundary, and feedback metrics — none of which belongs to this Type's core |\n187: | Project Management Application | plan/schedule-centric: tasks with dates, dependencies, and resources measured against a plan; a board may be one of its views |\n188: | Digital Whiteboard | a freeform spatial canvas with no card-state model; kanban templates on a whiteboard are optional task machinery, not this Type — remove position-as-state and allow free arrangement and it becomes a whiteboard |\n189: | Issue Tracker / Bug Tracking System | centers on the issue record and its admin-defined workflow schema and reporting; boards are a common view rather than the model of record |\n190: | Sales Pipeline Management | identical board grammar applied to a different world model (deals, buyers, values, win/loss) — sharing a surface grammar does not make one Type |\n191: | Work Management / Team Workspace Platforms | broad containers that may include boards as one module; embedding is packaging, not Type identity |\n192: \n193: The boundary worth remembering: the question is never \"does the product have a kanban view?\" but \"is the board where the work state lives?\" If the board is a rendering over a task table, the product belongs to task management; if the board is the record, it is a Kanban Task Board.\n194: \n195: ## Representative Products\n196: \n197: - Trello — general-purpose board-first archetype (personal free tier through enterprise)\n198: - Kanban Tool — SMB/team pure-play with kanban-method emphasis and time tracking\n199: - Businessmap (formerly Kanbanize) — enterprise lean platform carrying boards inside a portfolio/OKR suite\n200: - Wekan — open-source, self-hosted board tool\n201: \n202: The defining core was checked against the physical whiteboard antecedent and the open-source minimal form to avoid defining the Type by today's cloud-and-AI packaging.\n203: \n204: ## Sources\n205: \n206: Research date: **2026-09-08**\n207: \n208: - Trello — product tour and board-basics guide: https://trello.com/tour , https://trello.com/guide , https://trello.com/guide/trello-101\n209: - Kanban Tool — product functionality page and \"What is a Kanban Board?\" guide: https://kanbantool.com/product , https://kanbantool.com/kanban-guide/kanban-board , https://kanbantool.com/\n210: - Businessmap — product root and Kanban Boards feature page (incl. swimlane/sub-column FAQ): https://businessmap.io/ , https://businessmap.io/kanban-boards\n211: - Wekan — official README (\"Open Source kanban\"): https://github.com/wekan/wekan\n212: \n213: > Sourcing limitation: official support-article layers were not reachable for all sampled products this pass (Trello's Atlassian support center, Businessmap's knowledge base, and Wekan's feature wiki were not fetched). Operational specifics (exact plan gating, configuration mechanics, card-field enumerations for the open-source sample) are intentionally not asserted in this document; detailed evidence and calibration live in the paired Research Notes.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/milin/2026/软件开发/application-atlas/docs/template-factory/go-minimax-batch-2026-10-21/personal-kanban/calls/blueprint-spec-review-aux/leaf.md, lines 1-213\n1: # Kanban Task Board\n2: \n3: ## Overview\n4: \n5: A **Kanban Task Board** is a work-management application whose world is a visual board: work is represented as cards, cards are held in ordered columns, and a card's position on the board is the record of where that work stands. Moving a card from column to column is how progress happens and how progress is seen.\n6: \n7: The defining core is small:\n8: \n9: ```text\n10: Board (the organizing surface: ordered columns)\n11: └── Card (the unit of work)\n12:     └── Position-as-state (column membership = the card's status;\n13:         moving the card = the state change; column order = progression\n14:         toward done; card order = sequence/priority)\n15: ```\n16: \n17: Everything else the market associates with the category — card details, team collaboration, WIP limits, swimlanes, templates, automation, analytics, extra views — is standard or optional structure layered on that core, not what defines it. The software's ancestry is a physical whiteboard or wall with card notes; the product persists, shares, and scales that grammar. When the primary working model shifts away from the board — to a flat checklist, to task records with status fields viewed in lists, or to an ordered backlog driving timeboxed delivery — the product has crossed into a neighboring Application Type.\n18: \n19: ## Users & Context\n20: \n21: Two user populations share one structure:\n22: \n23: - **Individuals** use a board as a personal work surface: a private to-do flow (to-do → doing → done), a plan being assembled, a small project. A single-user board is a first-class form, not a degenerate case.\n24: - **Team members** work from a shared board that mirrors the team's own process. Members move cards as they take up and finish work; the board is the team's shared picture of who is doing what.\n25: - **Stakeholders and managers** largely read the board rather than operate it: status at a glance, where work is piling up, what is blocked or aging.\n26: \n27: The typical context is an ongoing flow of work rather than a fixed plan: requests and tasks arrive, get taken up, move through a few recognizable stages, and complete. Boards serve many settings — product and engineering teams, marketing and operations, sales processes, personal productivity — because the columns are defined by the users, not by the product.\n28: \n29: ## Core Model\n30: \n31: ### The Defining Core\n32: \n33: **The board.** The board is the primary working surface and the model of record — not one view over some other task database. A board usually frames one process, team, project, or purpose. It is divided into vertical **columns**, arranged left to right in the order the users' process unfolds.\n34: \n35: **The column (list / stage).** A named container for cards at the same point of the process. Column names come from the users' own workflow — backlog-ish, in-progress-ish, review-ish, done-ish — and are freely configurable: renamed, added, reordered, split. Column membership is meaningful: it is where the work stands. Columns may be subdivided into sub-columns for finer steps, and may carry capacity rules (see WIP limits under standard capabilities). Horizontal rows — **swimlanes** — can cross-cut the columns to separate types of work or classes of service.\n36: \n37: **The card.** A self-contained unit of work: a task, request, deliverable, or item to handle. A card carries its own details on its face and inside — title, description, assignee or members, due date, labels or colors, attachments, checklists, comments, and a history of what happened to it. Cards are movable; that movability is the point.\n38: \n39: **Position as state.** The card's column membership is the status. There is no separate status field that must be kept in sync: to change the state of the work, you move the card. Column order expresses progression from not-started toward done; the order of cards within a column expresses sequence or priority. A completed card either rests in a done column, gets marked complete, or is moved to an archive — the board's terminal handling keeps finished work out of the way without destroying it.\n40: \n41: ### Concept and Implementation\n42: \n43: The core is written conceptually; products realize it with different vocabularies:\n44: \n45: ```text\n46: Concept:   Board        → implementations:  board, project board, workflow board\n47: Concept:   Column       → implementations:  list, lane stage, workflow state column,\n48:                                             sub-columns for finer steps\n49: Concept:   Card         → implementations:  card, task card, work item card\n50: Concept:   Position     → implementations:  column membership (+ optional completion\n51:             as state                       flag, archive move, in-column ordering)\n52: ```\n53: \n54: A reader who has only seen one product's vocabulary should still recognize any other board tool from this model.\n55: \n56: ### Standard Capabilities\n57: \n58: Mature products almost universally add:\n59: \n60: - **Card detail** — description, assignee/members, due dates, labels or colors, attachments, checklists/subtasks, comments with notifications, per-card activity history.\n61: - **Real-time collaboration** — several people working the same board with live updates.\n62: - **Board membership and permissions** — who can see or edit a board; role tiers on larger accounts; per-board access levels.\n63: - **Workflow customization** — rename/add/reorder columns, sub-columns, collapsible columns and swimlanes, board backgrounds.\n64: - **Search and filtering** — find cards across the board (and the archive) by text, member, label, date.\n65: - **Board templates and cloning** — start from a prebuilt flow (task tracking, sales pipeline, product development) or duplicate an existing board.\n66: - **Automation** — rules that move, label, or otherwise act on cards when triggers occur (e.g., \"when a card moves to review, assign a reviewer\").\n67: - **Archiving** — completed cards leave the board but remain searchable.\n68: - **Notifications** — assignments, mentions, and due-date reminders, typically via email or in-app.\n69: - **API, integrations, and mobile clients.**\n70: \n71: ### Optional / Segment-dependent Capabilities\n72: \n73: - **WIP limits** — a cap per column on how many cards it may hold, so the team finishes started work before pulling more. Method-standard for kanban practice and prominent in methodology-first products; not present natively in every general-purpose tool.\n74: - **Swimlanes** — present in many team-oriented products, absent from the simplest forms.\n75: - **Flow analytics** — cumulative flow diagrams, cycle/lead time, aging, forecasting from historical data. The signature of methodology-first and enterprise offerings.\n76: - **Time tracking** — timers and reports attached to cards (a differentiator of some products rather than the norm).\n77: - **Secondary views** — calendar, timeline, table, or dashboard renderings of the same cards.\n78: - **Cross-board machinery** — moving cards between boards, linked board networks, portfolio layers above boards in enterprise suites.\n79: - **AI assistance** — text drafting, board construction from a description, or agents that execute cards on the board (current-generation layer).\n80: \n81: ## How It Works\n82: \n83: ### Shape the board\n84: \n85: ```text\n86: Create a board\n87: → name the columns after the real process (often starting from a template:\n88:    \"to do / doing / done\", or a richer request → in progress → review → done)\n89: → optionally split columns, add swimlanes, set WIP limits\n90: → invite members, set who can view/edit\n91: ```\n92: \n93: The board is configured, not programmed. As the process changes, the columns change — the same vendor guidance everywhere: the board should mirror how the team actually works.\n94: \n95: ### Fill and work the flow\n96: \n97: ```text\n98: Cards enter the board (added by hand, captured from requests/integrations,\n99:    or imported in bulk)\n100: → a member picks up a card (usually from the top of the first column)\n101: → moves it into the working column\n102: → does the work; discusses and attaches material on the card\n103: → moves it onward through review/delivery stages\n104: → it reaches done and is archived or left to rest\n105: ```\n106: \n107: This is a pull loop: people take up work as they free themselves, ideally finishing what they have before starting more. Where WIP limits are configured, the overfilled column becomes visible as a stop signal; how strictly the software enforces the cap varies by product. Dragging the card is not decoration — it *is* the status update, performed by the person doing the work, which is why the board can stay current without a reporting ceremony.\n108: \n109: ### Steer by looking\n110: \n111: The board is designed to be read at a distance: a glance shows what is on deck, what is in motion, what is done, and where cards accumulate. Teams use it in their regular check-ins; bottlenecks show up as fat columns, stalled or blocked work as aging cards, and overload as columns pressed against their limits. Managers and stakeholders read the same surface instead of asking for status.\n112: \n113: ### Adjust over time\n114: \n115: Boards are living structures: columns get renamed, merged, or sliced as the process is refined; cards get moved between boards; finished boards are archived. The improvement loop — look at flow, change the board, work the new flow — is part of the product's normal operation.\n116: \n117: ### Defining core vs standard vs optional\n118: \n119: - **Defining core** — board, cards, position-as-state, movement as the state change.\n120: - **Standard capabilities** — card detail, real-time collaboration, permissions, workflow customization, search/filter, templates, automation, archive, notifications, API/mobile.\n121: - **Optional / variant** — WIP limits, swimlanes, flow analytics, time tracking, secondary views, cross-board linking, AI, deployment choices, personal vs team orientation.\n122: \n123: ## Interfaces\n124: \n125: ### The board surface\n126: \n127: The product's main screen.\n128: \n129: - shows the board: columns left to right, swimlanes across, cards stacked in each column\n130: - cards display a summary face (title, colors/labels, members, due date, checklist progress)\n131: - primary actions: add a card, drag a card within or across columns, filter/search, open a card, collapse areas, open the board menu\n132: \n133: ### Card detail view\n134: \n135: Opens from any card.\n136: \n137: - description, checklist with progress, members, dates, labels, attachments, comments, activity history\n138: - primary actions: edit details, assign, comment, attach, move (or drag from here), copy/move to another board, archive or delete\n139: \n140: ### Board menu / settings\n141: \n142: The board's control panel.\n143: \n144: - members and permissions, background and appearance, automation rules, power-ups/extensions, activity feed, archive access\n145: - primary actions: manage access, configure the workflow (columns, swimlanes, WIP limits), set up automations, search the board\n146: \n147: ### Boards home / switcher\n148: \n149: The entry surface listing a user's boards and available templates.\n150: \n151: - board previews, favorites, team/workspace groupings, template gallery\n152: - primary actions: create a board, start from a template, open a recent board\n153: \n154: ### Secondary surfaces (where offered)\n155: \n156: Calendar, timeline, table, or dashboard renderings of the same cards; analytics screens (cumulative flow, cycle time, aging) in methodology-oriented products.\n157: \n158: ## Important Rules / Behaviors\n159: \n160: - **Position is the status.** The card's column membership is the authoritative state of the work. There is no second status field to reconcile; the board cannot lie about where work stands unless someone fails to move a card.\n161: - **The workflow is user-defined.** The product fixes no particular set of stages. Column names, count, and meaning are the users' own; two boards in the same product can model entirely different processes.\n162: - **Movement is the update operation.** Dragging a card between columns — with the position confirmed in the card's history — is how state changes are made; some products let rules automate specific moves.\n163: - **Capacity rules bind when configured.** A WIP limit makes an overfull column visually (and sometimes mechanically) resist new cards. The pull discipline — finish before starting more — is a practice the board supports, not one software generally enforces.\n164: - **Card order carries sequence.** Within a column, top-to-bottom order serves as the working priority, and members commonly take up cards from the top.\n165: - **Terminal work is preserved, not erased.** Completion moves cards to a done state or an archive; history remains inspectable, and the archive is searchable.\n166: - **Access is scoped per board.** A board has its own member/permission scope; boards can be private, team-shared, or broadly visible depending on the product's model.\n167: - **Boards are cheap to create and disposable by design.** One process or purpose per board; proliferation is expected and handled with a home/switcher surface.\n168: \n169: ## Variants\n170: \n171: - **Personal kanban boards** — single-user boards for private to-dos and planning; the simplest full form of the Type.\n172: - **Team process boards** — shared boards with permissions, swimlanes, and automation; the center of gravity of the market.\n173: - **General-purpose board-first tools** — the archetype: any workflow, any purpose, boards as the whole product.\n174: - **Methodology-first products** — lean/kanban-practice tools that foreground WIP limits, explicit policies, and flow metrics.\n175: - **Boards as a module** — board functionality embedded in task-management, workspace, or project-portfolio suites; the board's own model is unchanged by the packaging.\n176: - **Enterprise deployments** — linked board networks, portfolio layers above boards, enterprise access governance.\n177: - **Deployment forms** — cloud service, self-hosted commercial, open-source self-hosted.\n178: - **Secondary board uses** — idea boards, information and resource hubs: the same surface applied where the \"flow\" degenerates to loose categories.\n179: \n180: ## Related Application Types\n181: \n182: | Application Type | Distinction |\n183: |---|---|\n184: | To-do List Application | centers on a flat checklist with completion ticks and reminders; no board grammar — remove the columns and position-as-state and this Type collapses into it |\n185: | Task Management Application | centers on task *records* with status fields, priority, and list/calendar views; a kanban board there is one projection view over the records, whereas here the board is the native model and position is the state of record — the sharpest seam in this cluster |\n186: | Agile Project Management Application | the board is one surface of that Type; its defining structure adds a team-owned ordered backlog, a bounded delivery cadence (timeboxed iteration or continuous pull) as the re-planning boundary, and feedback metrics — none of which belongs to this Type's core |\n187: | Project Management Application | plan/schedule-centric: tasks with dates, dependencies, and resources measured against a plan; a board may be one of its views |\n188: | Digital Whiteboard | a freeform spatial canvas with no card-state model; kanban templates on a whiteboard are optional task machinery, not this Type — remove position-as-state and allow free arrangement and it becomes a whiteboard |\n189: | Issue Tracker / Bug Tracking System | centers on the issue record and its admin-defined workflow schema and reporting; boards are a common view rather than the model of record |\n190: | Sales Pipeline Management | identical board grammar applied to a different world model (deals, buyers, values, win/loss) — sharing a surface grammar does not make one Type |\n191: | Work Management / Team Workspace Platforms | broad containers that may include boards as one module; embedding is packaging, not Type identity |\n192: \n193: The boundary worth remembering: the question is never \"does the product have a kanban view?\" but \"is the board where the work state lives?\" If the board is a rendering over a task table, the product belongs to task management; if the board is the record, it is a Kanban Task Board.\n194: \n195: ## Representative Products\n196: \n197: - Trello — general-purpose board-first archetype (personal free tier through enterprise)\n198: - Kanban Tool — SMB/team pure-play with kanban-method emphasis and time tracking\n199: - Businessmap (formerly Kanbanize) — enterprise lean platform carrying boards inside a portfolio/OKR suite\n200: - Wekan — open-source, self-hosted board tool\n201: \n202: The defining core was checked against the physical whiteboard antecedent and the open-source minimal form to avoid defining the Type by today's cloud-and-AI packaging.\n203: \n204: ## Sources\n205: \n206: Research date: **2026-09-08**\n207: \n208: - Trello — product tour and board-basics guide: https://trello.com/tour , https://trello.com/guide , https://trello.com/guide/trello-101\n209: - Kanban Tool — product functionality page and \"What is a Kanban Board?\" guide: https://kanbantool.com/product , https://kanbantool.com/kanban-guide/kanban-board , https://kanbantool.com/\n210: - Businessmap — product root and Kanban Boards feature page (incl. swimlane/sub-column FAQ): https://businessmap.io/ , https://businessmap.io/kanban-boards\n211: - Wekan — official README (\"Open Source kanban\"): https://github.com/wekan/wekan\n212: \n213: > Sourcing limitation: official support-article layers were not reachable for all sampled products this pass (Trello's Atlassian support center, Businessmap's knowledge base, and Wekan's feature wiki were not fetched). Operational specifics (exact plan gating, configuration mechanics, card-field enumerations for the open-source sample) are intentionally not asserted in this document; detailed evidence and calibration live in the paired Research Notes."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/milin/2026/软件开发/application-atlas/docs/template-factory/go-minimax-batch-2026-10-21/personal-kanban/calls/blueprint-spec-review-aux/leaf.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1792580628563,"end":1792580628765}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792580628766,"sessionID":"ses_ea1de83daffetTStmPb5FvGcdC","part":{"partID":"prt_15e21b054001hjxdFh7HlRXXyD","sessionID":"ses_ea1de83daffetTStmPb5FvGcdC","messageID":"msg_15e21a691001AomlTBL2pTgu0A","type":"tool","id":"call_0ddc58799e1944de99eb23fb","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/milin/2026/软件开发/application-atlas/docs/template-factory/go-minimax-batch-2026-10-21/personal-kanban/calls/blueprint-spec-review-aux/source/candidate/REVIEW_SCOPE.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/milin/2026/软件开发/application-atlas/docs/template-factory/go-minimax-batch-2026-10-21/personal-kanban/calls/blueprint-spec-review-aux/source/candidate/REVIEW_SCOPE.md, lines 1-365\n1: ## workflow\n2: \n3: ### 0\n4: \"1. Operator starts the process with `python3 app.py --data-dir ./data --host 127.0.0.1 --port\n5: 8765`; the data directory and the SQLite file are created on first run and the startup banner prints\n6: the local URL.\"\n7: \n8: ### 1\n9: \"2. The user opens `GET /` (boards home) and creates a named board, optionally seeding user-named\n10: columns (for example 待办 / 进行中 / 完成) in one step; the board is stored with zero hidden default\n11: columns.\"\n12: \n13: ### 2\n14: \"3. The user adds further columns, renames a column in place, and reorders columns to match the real\n15: workflow; the column order is stored as a contiguous 1..N position sequence and is always rendered\n16: left to right.\"\n17: \n18: ### 3\n19: \"4. The user creates a card with a title and a short description inside a chosen column; the card is\n20: appended at the bottom of that column unless an explicit slot is given.\"\n21: \n22: ### 4\n23: \"5. The user edits a card's title or description; editing never changes the card's column, position\n24: or moved timestamp.\"\n25: \n26: ### 5\n27: \"6. The user moves a card to another column (this is the state change - there is no status field)\n28: and reorders cards inside a column; the source column and target column are renumbered to contiguous\n29: 1..N in one transaction.\"\n30: \n31: ### 6\n32: \"7. The user reads the board surface: columns left to right, each column showing its full ordered\n33: card list and its card count, with the board total equal to the sum of the column counts.\"\n34: \n35: ### 7\n36: \"8. The user restarts the process against the same `--data-dir` and sees the same boards, the same\n37: column order and the same card order, because every write is committed to `<data-\n38: dir>/kanban.sqlite3`.\"\n39: \n40: ### 8\n41: \"9. The acceptance probe (one disposable board per case, deleting only what it created) exercises\n42: the same journey over the JSON API and asserts the zero-write behaviour of every rejected request.\"\n43: \n44: ## acceptance\n45: \n46: ### 0\n47: \"The probe runs against a live target with `python3 probe.py --base-url http://127.0.0.1:8765 --case\n48: A01` (or `--case all`, repeatable `--case` flags) and exits 0 only when every selected case passes;\n49: the five cases A01..A05 map one-to-one onto the five features.\"\n50: \n51: ### 1\n52: \"A01 proves board behaviour: POST /api/boards creates exactly one board (201 with id, name,\n53: created_at, updated_at, column_count, card_count), GET /api/boards grows by exactly one entry and\n54: carries the same name and counts, GET /api/boards/{id} returns the same board with an empty column\n55: list, PATCH renames without changing the id, and DELETE removes exactly that board.\"\n56: \n57: ### 2\n58: \"A02 proves column behaviour: seeded columns keep their names and order with positions 1..3, a new\n59: column is appended at position 4, a rename keeps the position, a reorder persists the new order with\n60: positions exactly 1..4 and names still attached to the same ids, and deleting an empty column\n61: renumbers the remaining columns to 1..N.\"\n62: \n63: ### 3\n64: \"A03 proves card behaviour: a card is created with title, description, column and position 1, a\n65: second card gets position 2, the board's card_count and the per-column card_count equal the real\n66: number of cards, and PATCH changes only title/description (position, column and moved_at\n67: unchanged).\"\n68: \n69: ### 4\n70: \"Every rejected request in A01..A04 is followed by a re-read that proves zero writes: the\n71: board/column/card counts, the column order, the card order, the card positions and each card's\n72: updated_at are byte-for-byte the same before and after the rejection (board_state() comparison).\"\n73: \n74: ### 5\n75: \"Negative cases are exact: blank or over-long name, blank or over-long title, over-long description,\n76: missing column, unknown column (404 COLUMN_NOT_FOUND), a column of another board (422\n77: COLUMN_BOARD_MISMATCH), position 0 or position beyond the last slot (422 POSITION_OUT_OF_RANGE),\n78: duplicate column name (409 DUPLICATE_COLUMN_NAME), deleting a non-empty column (409\n79: COLUMN_NOT_EMPTY), deleting the last column (409 LAST_COLUMN), malformed JSON body (400\n80: MALFORMED_JSON), and forbidden P0 keys such as status, assignee, due_date, recurrence_trigger,\n81: task_limit (422 FORBIDDEN_FIELD) or colour (422 UNKNOWN_FIELD).\"\n82: \n83: ### 6\n84: \"A04 proves position-as-state: moving a card to another column renumbers the source column to 1..M-1\n85: and the target column to 1..N, an in-column reorder swaps the two cards and their positions, the\n86: board's total card count is unchanged by every move, moved_at never precedes created_at, and a no-op\n87: move leaves the column order untouched.\"\n88: \n89: ### 7\n90: \"A05 proves the read model and the date edges: GET /api/boards/{id} returns the columns in position\n91: order, each with its full ordered card list, a card_count equal to the length of that list,\n92: contiguous card positions starting at 1, a board card_count equal to the sum of the column counts,\n93: the expected exact set of card titles per column, an identical snapshot on a second read, and\n94: created_at <= updated_at and created_at <= moved_at for every card inside the probe's write window.\"\n95: \n96: ### 8\n97: \"Cross-process restart durability is specified in README.md as an operator procedure (stop the\n98: process, start it again with the same --data-dir, re-read the board) because the probe cannot stop\n99: or start the server; the blueprint claims that step was not executed, and the whole runtime\n100: verification of this target is not_run.\"\n101: \n102: ### 9\n103: \"The probe only uses records it created in its own disposable board and deletes only those records;\n104: it never deletes pre-existing user data, and it asserts only behaviour documented in this\n105: architecture - no upstream Kanboard behaviour is asserted.\"\n106: \n107: ## observations\n108: \n109: ### 0\n110: [\"documentation\", \"README.md\", 4, 14, \"Upstream README describes Kanboard as project management\n111: software that focuses on the Kanban methodology and states the application is in maintenance mode:\n112: the author is not actively developing any new major features, only small fixes are made, and pull\n113: requests for new features and bug fixes are accepted.\"]\n114: \n115: ### 1\n116: [\"documentation\", \"README.md\", 19, 27, \"Upstream README links the official website, a feature list,\n117: the change log, a forum and the documentation pages for requirements, installation, upgrade and\n118: Docker use, i.e. upstream is a full server product with its own documentation and deployment story,\n119: not a local single-user tool.\"]\n120: \n121: ### 2\n122: [\"documentation\", \"README.md\", 29, 34, \"Upstream README names Frederic Guillot as main developer,\n123: points at the contributor list and states that the project is distributed under the MIT License.\"]\n124: \n125: ### 3\n126: [\"documentation\", \"LICENSE\", 1, 21, \"The pinned upstream LICENSE is the MIT License (MIT), copyright\n127: (c) Frederic Guillot, with the standard grant, the notice requirement and the warranty disclaimer.\"]\n128: \n129: ### 4\n130: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 183, 195, \"Upstream create() persists a new column\n131: for a project with title, task_limit (a WIP cap), description and hide_in_dashboard, and sets\n132: position to getLastColumnPosition(project_id) + 1, so a newly added column is appended at the end of\n133: the board rather than inserted at an arbitrary slot.\"]\n134: \n135: ### 5\n136: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 208, 216, \"Upstream update() rewrites the column\n137: title, task_limit, hide_in_dashboard and description in place and never touches the column position,\n138: so renaming a column is independent of the column order.\"]\n139: \n140: ### 6\n141: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 239, 261, \"Upstream changePosition() rejects a\n142: position below 1 or above the number of columns of the project, then renumbers the sibling columns\n143: ascending by their current position and finally writes the requested position on the moved column,\n144: returning false if any single update fails - reordering is a bounded, all-or-nothing rewrite of the\n145: sibling positions.\"]\n146: \n147: ### 7\n148: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 118, 121, \"Upstream getAll() returns the columns\n149: of a project ordered ascending by position, which is the mechanism behind the left-to-right column\n150: order of the board surface.\"]\n151: \n152: ### 8\n153: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 147, 156, \"Upstream getAllWithTaskCount() returns\n154: the columns ordered by position together with a per-column count of open tasks (is_active='1') and\n155: of closed tasks (is_active='0'), i.e. upstream derives the column card counts from the task table\n156: itself.\"]\n157: \n158: ### 9\n159: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 225, 228, \"Upstream remove() deletes a column row\n160: by id and its docblock states that the column and all associated tasks are removed, so upstream\n161: column deletion is destructive rather than a move of the cards elsewhere.\"]\n162: \n163: ### 10\n164: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 66, 97, \"Upstream movePosition() returns\n165: false for a position below 1, resolves the swimlane when none is given, skips closed tasks when\n166: onlyOpen is set, and then dispatches to exactly one of three branches - swimlane change, column\n167: change or same-column position change - so a card's placement is the only way its location\n168: changes.\"]\n169: \n170: ### 11\n171: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 177, 215, \"Upstream saveTaskPositions()\n172: lists the other tasks of the same column ordered by position then id, inserts the moved task when\n173: the requested position equals the running offset, rewrites every other task's position, and finally\n174: places the task at the bottom while normalising a bad position - the whole column is renumbered\n175: around the moved card.\"]\n176: \n177: ### 12\n178: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 112, 121, \"Upstream saveSwimlaneChange()\n179: performs the source-column rewrite, the target-column rewrite and the timestamp update inside one\n180: database transaction and returns the conjunction of the three results, i.e. a move commits as a\n181: single all-or-nothing unit.\"]\n182: \n183: ### 13\n184: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 135, 144, \"Upstream saveColumnChange()\n185: applies the same transaction pattern for a move between two columns: clear the source column, fill\n186: the target column, update the timestamps, and report success only when all three writes succeeded.\"]\n187: \n188: ### 14\n189: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 244, 258, \"Upstream saveTaskPosition()\n190: cancels the surrounding transaction when the row update affects no rows, which is how a failed mid-\n191: move write aborts the whole move instead of leaving a half-renumbered column.\"]\n192: \n193: ### 15\n194: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 15, 51, \"Upstream moveBottom() computes\n195: MAX(position)+1 over the tasks of the target column and writes column_id, position, date_moved and\n196: date_modification, so appending a card to a column is a position write and not a separate status\n197: flag.\"]\n198: \n199: ### 16\n200: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 269, 313, \"Upstream fireEvents() emits a\n201: distinct event for a swimlane change, a column change or a position change and carries\n202: src_column_id, dst_column_id, date_moved and the task's recurrence fields; in upstream the move is\n203: the state-change event of the card.\"]\n204: \n205: ### 17\n206: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskCreationModel.php\", 61, 93, \"Upstream prepare() defaults an\n207: empty column_id to the first column of the project, defaults an empty title to 'Untitled', stamps\n208: date_creation, date_modification and date_moved with the current time and computes position as the\n209: number of tasks in the column plus one.\"]\n210: \n211: ### 18\n212: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskCreationModel.php\", 22, 53, \"Upstream create() persists the task\n213: row first and only afterwards calls movePosition() when an explicit position greater than one was\n214: requested, i.e. inserting a card at a chosen slot is a create step followed by a position step.\"]\n215: \n216: ### 19\n217: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskModificationModel.php\", 23, 41, \"Upstream update() applies the\n218: changed fields to the task row and fires the modification events only when the write succeeded; the\n219: card's text fields are edited independently of its column and position.\"]\n220: \n221: ### 20\n222: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskModificationModel.php\", 105, 117, \"Upstream prepare() converts\n223: date_due and date_started through the date parser, removes the id field, resets the date, score and\n224: time fields and stamps date_modification - upstream tasks carry due/start dates and recurrence\n225: counters, which the pinned P0 scenario deliberately excludes.\"]\n226: \n227: ### 21\n228: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Controller/TaskMovePositionController.php\", 33, 52, \"Upstream\n229: TaskMovePositionController::save() reads a JSON body with column_id, position and swimlane_id,\n230: checks a move permission on the project role, calls movePosition() with the project id, the task id\n231: and those three values, and then redirects back to the task view.\"]\n232: \n233: ### 22\n234: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Template/board/table_column.php\", 5, 27, \"The upstream board template\n235: renders one table header cell per column carrying data-column-id, the escaped column title and a\n236: per-column dropdown, so the column order of the board surface is exactly the column position\n237: order.\"]\n238: \n239: ### 23\n240: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Template/board/table_column.php\", 88, 120, \"The upstream column header\n241: renders the score, the visible task count and - when a task_limit is set - the total across\n242: swimlanes, i.e. upstream already shows a per-column card count next to the column title.\"]\n243: \n244: ### 24\n245: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Template/board/table_column.php\", 105, 119, \"The upstream task count block\n246: is rendered only when the count is non-empty and is preceded by an ui-helper-hidden-accessible 'Task\n247: count' text, i.e. upstream labels the column count for assistive technology.\"]\n248: \n249: ### 25\n250: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Template/board/table_column.php\", 39, 43, \"The upstream column dropdown\n251: offers 'Close all tasks in this column and this swimlane' only when the column holds tasks, showing\n252: that upstream treats the column as the container whose content can be closed in bulk - a batch\n253: operation outside the pinned P0 scenario.\"]\n254: \n255: ## features\n256: \n257: ### 0\n258: \"Named boards with durable local storage (create, list, read, rename, delete)\"\n259: \n260: ### 1\n261: \"User-named ordered columns (add, rename, reorder, guarded delete)\"\n262: \n263: ### 2\n264: \"Cards with a title and a short description (create, read, edit)\"\n265: \n266: ### 3\n267: \"Movement as state: cross-column move and in-column reorder with contiguous renumbering\"\n268: \n269: ### 4\n270: \"Board read model: columns in order, full per-column card lists and per-column counts\"\n271: \n272: ## goals\n273: \n274: ### 0\n275: \"A POST /api/boards with a 1..120 character name creates exactly one board row and answers 201 with\n276: id, name, created_at, updated_at, column_count and card_count; GET /api/boards then lists that board\n277: once with the same counts, GET /api/boards/{id} returns the same board with an empty column list,\n278: and after the process is restarted with the same --data-dir the same board id, name and counts are\n279: read back unchanged.\"\n280: \n281: ### 1\n282: \"Columns can be appended, renamed in place and reordered: after every accepted change the board's\n283: columns are exactly the contiguous permutation 1..N of their positions, GET /api/boards/{id} returns\n284: them in that order with every name still attached to the same column id, deleting an empty column\n285: renumbers the remaining columns to 1..N, and deleting a non-empty column (409 COLUMN_NOT_EMPTY) or\n286: the last column of a board (409 LAST_COLUMN) is rejected without writing.\"\n287: \n288: ### 2\n289: \"A card can be created in a chosen column with a required title of 1..200 characters and an optional\n290: description of at most 500 characters, is appended at the bottom of that column unless an explicit\n291: slot is requested, and PATCH can change only the title and the description - never the column, the\n292: position or moved_at; a rejected card write (blank or over-long title, over-long description,\n293: missing or unknown column, forbidden key such as status, due_date or recurrence_trigger, unknown key\n294: such as colour) leaves the card set, the card order, the per-column counts and every card's\n295: updated_at exactly unchanged.\"\n296: \n297: ### 3\n298: \"POST /api/cards/{id}/move with (column_id, position) places the card in the 1-based slot position\n299: of the target column, renumbers the source column to 1..M-1 and the target column to 1..N inside one\n300: transaction, keeps the board's total card count unchanged and bumps moved_at, while an out-of-range\n301: position, an unknown column, a column belonging to another board and an unknown card are all\n302: rejected without any write.\"\n303: \n304: ### 4\n305: \"GET /api/boards/{id} returns the board, its columns in position order and for every column the\n306: complete ordered list of its cards plus a card_count equal to the length of that list, so that the\n307: column counts sum to the board card_count, every card list carries contiguous positions starting at\n308: 1, the expected card titles appear exactly in the expected columns, and two consecutive reads return\n309: an identical snapshot.\"\n310: \n311: ## gaps\n312: \n313: ### 0\n314: \"Runtime verification status: not_run. No target server was started, no HTTP request was issued and\n315: no probe case was executed while producing this blueprint; the acceptance criteria state what must\n316: hold, not what was observed.\"\n317: \n318: ### 1\n319: \"Upstream status: not_run. The pinned Kanboard snapshot was read as source text only - it was not\n320: installed, started or exercised - so no upstream runtime behaviour, upstream database schema or\n321: upstream performance characteristic is asserted here.\"\n322: \n323: ### 2\n324: \"Only the eight pinned upstream files were read; the rest of the upstream tree (routing, migrations,\n325: templates, tests) is outside the evidence set, so claims about upstream beyond these files are not\n326: made.\"\n327: \n328: ### 3\n329: \"Restart durability is specified but not demonstrated: the probe cannot stop or start the server, so\n330: the cross-process restart check exists only as an operator procedure in README.md and is not claimed\n331: as executed.\"\n332: \n333: ### 4\n334: \"The probe compares API-level shapes only; it asserts nothing about the HTML surface, so the\n335: accessibility, Post/Redirect/Get and focus requirements of the UI are reviewed by hand rather than\n336: machine-checked.\"\n337: \n338: ### 5\n339: \"Drag-and-drop is deliberately out of P0: the UI exposes movement through buttons and a select, so\n340: pointer-only drag affordances, live drag feedback and cross-column drag previews are not covered.\"\n341: \n342: ### 6\n343: \"The target stores no due dates, recurrence rules, assignees, labels, priorities, attachments,\n344: comments, checklists, activity history or archive, so the leaf's 'card detail' and 'standard\n345: capabilities' layers are absent by design in P0.\"\n346: \n347: ### 7\n348: \"Deletion is destructive without archive or undo, so a mistaken board deletion cannot be recovered\n349: in this P0 build (decision D5).\"\n350: \n351: ### 8\n352: \"The HTTP server is single-threaded and the probe runs sequentially, so concurrent writers, lock\n353: contention and busy_timeout behaviour are not exercised.\"\n354: \n355: ### 9\n356: \"No authentication, TLS, per-board permissions or multi-user isolation: the process must stay on the\n357: loopback interface.\"\n358: \n359: ### 10\n360: \"Column rename has no per-column history and card edits record no change log, so 'the position\n361: confirmed in the card's history' from the leaf is not implemented in P0.\"\n362: \n363: ### 11\n364: \"The upstream MIT licence covers the pinned reference files only; no upstream source was copied into\n365: the target, and a file-level licence audit of the upstream tree was not performed.\"","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/milin/2026/软件开发/application-atlas/docs/template-factory/go-minimax-batch-2026-10-21/personal-kanban/calls/blueprint-spec-review-aux/source/candidate/REVIEW_SCOPE.md, lines 1-365\n1: ## workflow\n2: \n3: ### 0\n4: \"1. Operator starts the process with `python3 app.py --data-dir ./data --host 127.0.0.1 --port\n5: 8765`; the data directory and the SQLite file are created on first run and the startup banner prints\n6: the local URL.\"\n7: \n8: ### 1\n9: \"2. The user opens `GET /` (boards home) and creates a named board, optionally seeding user-named\n10: columns (for example 待办 / 进行中 / 完成) in one step; the board is stored with zero hidden default\n11: columns.\"\n12: \n13: ### 2\n14: \"3. The user adds further columns, renames a column in place, and reorders columns to match the real\n15: workflow; the column order is stored as a contiguous 1..N position sequence and is always rendered\n16: left to right.\"\n17: \n18: ### 3\n19: \"4. The user creates a card with a title and a short description inside a chosen column; the card is\n20: appended at the bottom of that column unless an explicit slot is given.\"\n21: \n22: ### 4\n23: \"5. The user edits a card's title or description; editing never changes the card's column, position\n24: or moved timestamp.\"\n25: \n26: ### 5\n27: \"6. The user moves a card to another column (this is the state change - there is no status field)\n28: and reorders cards inside a column; the source column and target column are renumbered to contiguous\n29: 1..N in one transaction.\"\n30: \n31: ### 6\n32: \"7. The user reads the board surface: columns left to right, each column showing its full ordered\n33: card list and its card count, with the board total equal to the sum of the column counts.\"\n34: \n35: ### 7\n36: \"8. The user restarts the process against the same `--data-dir` and sees the same boards, the same\n37: column order and the same card order, because every write is committed to `<data-\n38: dir>/kanban.sqlite3`.\"\n39: \n40: ### 8\n41: \"9. The acceptance probe (one disposable board per case, deleting only what it created) exercises\n42: the same journey over the JSON API and asserts the zero-write behaviour of every rejected request.\"\n43: \n44: ## acceptance\n45: \n46: ### 0\n47: \"The probe runs against a live target with `python3 probe.py --base-url http://127.0.0.1:8765 --case\n48: A01` (or `--case all`, repeatable `--case` flags) and exits 0 only when every selected case passes;\n49: the five cases A01..A05 map one-to-one onto the five features.\"\n50: \n51: ### 1\n52: \"A01 proves board behaviour: POST /api/boards creates exactly one board (201 with id, name,\n53: created_at, updated_at, column_count, card_count), GET /api/boards grows by exactly one entry and\n54: carries the same name and counts, GET /api/boards/{id} returns the same board with an empty column\n55: list, PATCH renames without changing the id, and DELETE removes exactly that board.\"\n56: \n57: ### 2\n58: \"A02 proves column behaviour: seeded columns keep their names and order with positions 1..3, a new\n59: column is appended at position 4, a rename keeps the position, a reorder persists the new order with\n60: positions exactly 1..4 and names still attached to the same ids, and deleting an empty column\n61: renumbers the remaining columns to 1..N.\"\n62: \n63: ### 3\n64: \"A03 proves card behaviour: a card is created with title, description, column and position 1, a\n65: second card gets position 2, the board's card_count and the per-column card_count equal the real\n66: number of cards, and PATCH changes only title/description (position, column and moved_at\n67: unchanged).\"\n68: \n69: ### 4\n70: \"Every rejected request in A01..A04 is followed by a re-read that proves zero writes: the\n71: board/column/card counts, the column order, the card order, the card positions and each card's\n72: updated_at are byte-for-byte the same before and after the rejection (board_state() comparison).\"\n73: \n74: ### 5\n75: \"Negative cases are exact: blank or over-long name, blank or over-long title, over-long description,\n76: missing column, unknown column (404 COLUMN_NOT_FOUND), a column of another board (422\n77: COLUMN_BOARD_MISMATCH), position 0 or position beyond the last slot (422 POSITION_OUT_OF_RANGE),\n78: duplicate column name (409 DUPLICATE_COLUMN_NAME), deleting a non-empty column (409\n79: COLUMN_NOT_EMPTY), deleting the last column (409 LAST_COLUMN), malformed JSON body (400\n80: MALFORMED_JSON), and forbidden P0 keys such as status, assignee, due_date, recurrence_trigger,\n81: task_limit (422 FORBIDDEN_FIELD) or colour (422 UNKNOWN_FIELD).\"\n82: \n83: ### 6\n84: \"A04 proves position-as-state: moving a card to another column renumbers the source column to 1..M-1\n85: and the target column to 1..N, an in-column reorder swaps the two cards and their positions, the\n86: board's total card count is unchanged by every move, moved_at never precedes created_at, and a no-op\n87: move leaves the column order untouched.\"\n88: \n89: ### 7\n90: \"A05 proves the read model and the date edges: GET /api/boards/{id} returns the columns in position\n91: order, each with its full ordered card list, a card_count equal to the length of that list,\n92: contiguous card positions starting at 1, a board card_count equal to the sum of the column counts,\n93: the expected exact set of card titles per column, an identical snapshot on a second read, and\n94: created_at <= updated_at and created_at <= moved_at for every card inside the probe's write window.\"\n95: \n96: ### 8\n97: \"Cross-process restart durability is specified in README.md as an operator procedure (stop the\n98: process, start it again with the same --data-dir, re-read the board) because the probe cannot stop\n99: or start the server; the blueprint claims that step was not executed, and the whole runtime\n100: verification of this target is not_run.\"\n101: \n102: ### 9\n103: \"The probe only uses records it created in its own disposable board and deletes only those records;\n104: it never deletes pre-existing user data, and it asserts only behaviour documented in this\n105: architecture - no upstream Kanboard behaviour is asserted.\"\n106: \n107: ## observations\n108: \n109: ### 0\n110: [\"documentation\", \"README.md\", 4, 14, \"Upstream README describes Kanboard as project management\n111: software that focuses on the Kanban methodology and states the application is in maintenance mode:\n112: the author is not actively developing any new major features, only small fixes are made, and pull\n113: requests for new features and bug fixes are accepted.\"]\n114: \n115: ### 1\n116: [\"documentation\", \"README.md\", 19, 27, \"Upstream README links the official website, a feature list,\n117: the change log, a forum and the documentation pages for requirements, installation, upgrade and\n118: Docker use, i.e. upstream is a full server product with its own documentation and deployment story,\n119: not a local single-user tool.\"]\n120: \n121: ### 2\n122: [\"documentation\", \"README.md\", 29, 34, \"Upstream README names Frederic Guillot as main developer,\n123: points at the contributor list and states that the project is distributed under the MIT License.\"]\n124: \n125: ### 3\n126: [\"documentation\", \"LICENSE\", 1, 21, \"The pinned upstream LICENSE is the MIT License (MIT), copyright\n127: (c) Frederic Guillot, with the standard grant, the notice requirement and the warranty disclaimer.\"]\n128: \n129: ### 4\n130: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 183, 195, \"Upstream create() persists a new column\n131: for a project with title, task_limit (a WIP cap), description and hide_in_dashboard, and sets\n132: position to getLastColumnPosition(project_id) + 1, so a newly added column is appended at the end of\n133: the board rather than inserted at an arbitrary slot.\"]\n134: \n135: ### 5\n136: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 208, 216, \"Upstream update() rewrites the column\n137: title, task_limit, hide_in_dashboard and description in place and never touches the column position,\n138: so renaming a column is independent of the column order.\"]\n139: \n140: ### 6\n141: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 239, 261, \"Upstream changePosition() rejects a\n142: position below 1 or above the number of columns of the project, then renumbers the sibling columns\n143: ascending by their current position and finally writes the requested position on the moved column,\n144: returning false if any single update fails - reordering is a bounded, all-or-nothing rewrite of the\n145: sibling positions.\"]\n146: \n147: ### 7\n148: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 118, 121, \"Upstream getAll() returns the columns\n149: of a project ordered ascending by position, which is the mechanism behind the left-to-right column\n150: order of the board surface.\"]\n151: \n152: ### 8\n153: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 147, 156, \"Upstream getAllWithTaskCount() returns\n154: the columns ordered by position together with a per-column count of open tasks (is_active='1') and\n155: of closed tasks (is_active='0'), i.e. upstream derives the column card counts from the task table\n156: itself.\"]\n157: \n158: ### 9\n159: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/ColumnModel.php\", 225, 228, \"Upstream remove() deletes a column row\n160: by id and its docblock states that the column and all associated tasks are removed, so upstream\n161: column deletion is destructive rather than a move of the cards elsewhere.\"]\n162: \n163: ### 10\n164: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 66, 97, \"Upstream movePosition() returns\n165: false for a position below 1, resolves the swimlane when none is given, skips closed tasks when\n166: onlyOpen is set, and then dispatches to exactly one of three branches - swimlane change, column\n167: change or same-column position change - so a card's placement is the only way its location\n168: changes.\"]\n169: \n170: ### 11\n171: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 177, 215, \"Upstream saveTaskPositions()\n172: lists the other tasks of the same column ordered by position then id, inserts the moved task when\n173: the requested position equals the running offset, rewrites every other task's position, and finally\n174: places the task at the bottom while normalising a bad position - the whole column is renumbered\n175: around the moved card.\"]\n176: \n177: ### 12\n178: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 112, 121, \"Upstream saveSwimlaneChange()\n179: performs the source-column rewrite, the target-column rewrite and the timestamp update inside one\n180: database transaction and returns the conjunction of the three results, i.e. a move commits as a\n181: single all-or-nothing unit.\"]\n182: \n183: ### 13\n184: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 135, 144, \"Upstream saveColumnChange()\n185: applies the same transaction pattern for a move between two columns: clear the source column, fill\n186: the target column, update the timestamps, and report success only when all three writes succeeded.\"]\n187: \n188: ### 14\n189: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 244, 258, \"Upstream saveTaskPosition()\n190: cancels the surrounding transaction when the row update affects no rows, which is how a failed mid-\n191: move write aborts the whole move instead of leaving a half-renumbered column.\"]\n192: \n193: ### 15\n194: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 15, 51, \"Upstream moveBottom() computes\n195: MAX(position)+1 over the tasks of the target column and writes column_id, position, date_moved and\n196: date_modification, so appending a card to a column is a position write and not a separate status\n197: flag.\"]\n198: \n199: ### 16\n200: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskPositionModel.php\", 269, 313, \"Upstream fireEvents() emits a\n201: distinct event for a swimlane change, a column change or a position change and carries\n202: src_column_id, dst_column_id, date_moved and the task's recurrence fields; in upstream the move is\n203: the state-change event of the card.\"]\n204: \n205: ### 17\n206: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskCreationModel.php\", 61, 93, \"Upstream prepare() defaults an\n207: empty column_id to the first column of the project, defaults an empty title to 'Untitled', stamps\n208: date_creation, date_modification and date_moved with the current time and computes position as the\n209: number of tasks in the column plus one.\"]\n210: \n211: ### 18\n212: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskCreationModel.php\", 22, 53, \"Upstream create() persists the task\n213: row first and only afterwards calls movePosition() when an explicit position greater than one was\n214: requested, i.e. inserting a card at a chosen slot is a create step followed by a position step.\"]\n215: \n216: ### 19\n217: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskModificationModel.php\", 23, 41, \"Upstream update() applies the\n218: changed fields to the task row and fires the modification events only when the write succeeded; the\n219: card's text fields are edited independently of its column and position.\"]\n220: \n221: ### 20\n222: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Model/TaskModificationModel.php\", 105, 117, \"Upstream prepare() converts\n223: date_due and date_started through the date parser, removes the id field, resets the date, score and\n224: time fields and stamps date_modification - upstream tasks carry due/start dates and recurrence\n225: counters, which the pinned P0 scenario deliberately excludes.\"]\n226: \n227: ### 21\n228: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Controller/TaskMovePositionController.php\", 33, 52, \"Upstream\n229: TaskMovePositionController::save() reads a JSON body with column_id, position and swimlane_id,\n230: checks a move permission on the project role, calls movePosition() with the project id, the task id\n231: and those three values, and then redirects back to the task view.\"]\n232: \n233: ### 22\n234: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Template/board/table_column.php\", 5, 27, \"The upstream board template\n235: renders one table header cell per column carrying data-column-id, the escaped column title and a\n236: per-column dropdown, so the column order of the board surface is exactly the column position\n237: order.\"]\n238: \n239: ### 23\n240: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Template/board/table_column.php\", 88, 120, \"The upstream column header\n241: renders the score, the visible task count and - when a task_limit is set - the total across\n242: swimlanes, i.e. upstream already shows a per-column card count next to the column title.\"]\n243: \n244: ### 24\n245: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Template/board/table_column.php\", 105, 119, \"The upstream task count block\n246: is rendered only when the count is non-empty and is preceded by an ui-helper-hidden-accessible 'Task\n247: count' text, i.e. upstream labels the column count for assistive technology.\"]\n248: \n249: ### 25\n250: [\"code_observation\", \"app/Template/board/table_column.php\", 39, 43, \"The upstream column dropdown\n251: offers 'Close all tasks in this column and this swimlane' only when the column holds tasks, showing\n252: that upstream treats the column as the container whose content can be closed in bulk - a batch\n253: operation outside the pinned P0 scenario.\"]\n254: \n255: ## features\n256: \n257: ### 0\n258: \"Named boards with durable local storage (create, list, read, rename, delete)\"\n259: \n260: ### 1\n261: \"User-named ordered columns (add, rename, reorder, guarded delete)\"\n262: \n263: ### 2\n264: \"Cards with a title and a short description (create, read, edit)\"\n265: \n266: ### 3\n267: \"Movement as state: cross-column move and in-column reorder with contiguous renumbering\"\n268: \n269: ### 4\n270: \"Board read model: columns in order, full per-column card lists and per-column counts\"\n271: \n272: ## goals\n273: \n274: ### 0\n275: \"A POST /api/boards with a 1..120 character name creates exactly one board row and answers 201 with\n276: id, name, created_at, updated_at, column_count and card_count; GET /api/boards then lists that board\n277: once with the same counts, GET /api/boards/{id} returns the same board with an empty column list,\n278: and after the process is restarted with the same --data-dir the same board id, name and counts are\n279: read back unchanged.\"\n280: \n281: ### 1\n282: \"Columns can be appended, renamed in place and reordered: after every accepted change the board's\n283: columns are exactly the contiguous permutation 1..N of their positions, GET /api/boards/{id} returns\n284: them in that order with every name still attached to the same column id, deleting an empty column\n285: renumbers the remaining columns to 1..N, and deleting a non-empty column (409 COLUMN_NOT_EMPTY) or\n286: the last column of a board (409 LAST_COLUMN) is rejected without writing.\"\n287: \n288: ### 2\n289: \"A card can be created in a chosen column with a required title of 1..200 characters and an optional\n290: description of at most 500 characters, is appended at the bottom of that column unless an explicit\n291: slot is requested, and PATCH can change only the title and the description - never the column, the\n292: position or moved_at; a rejected card write (blank or over-long title, over-long description,\n293: missing or unknown column, forbidden key such as status, due_date or recurrence_trigger, unknown key\n294: such as colour) leaves the card set, the card order, the per-column counts and every card's\n295: updated_at exactly unchanged.\"\n296: \n297: ### 3\n298: \"POST /api/cards/{id}/move with (column_id, position) places the card in the 1-based slot position\n299: of the target column, renumbers the source column to 1..M-1 and the target column to 1..N inside one\n300: transaction, keeps the board's total card count unchanged and bumps moved_at, while an out-of-range\n301: position, an unknown column, a column belonging to another board and an unknown card are all\n302: rejected without any write.\"\n303: \n304: ### 4\n305: \"GET /api/boards/{id} returns the board, its columns in position order and for every column the\n306: complete ordered list of its cards plus a card_count equal to the length of that list, so that the\n307: column counts sum to the board card_count, every card list carries contiguous positions starting at\n308: 1, the expected card titles appear exactly in the expected columns, and two consecutive reads return\n309: an identical snapshot.\"\n310: \n311: ## gaps\n312: \n313: ### 0\n314: \"Runtime verification status: not_run. No target server was started, no HTTP request was issued and\n315: no probe case was executed while producing this blueprint; the acceptance criteria state what must\n316: hold, not what was observed.\"\n317: \n318: ### 1\n319: \"Upstream status: not_run. The pinned Kanboard snapshot was read as source text only - it was not\n320: installed, started or exercised - so no upstream runtime behaviour, upstream database schema or\n321: upstream performance characteristic is asserted here.\"\n322: \n323: ### 2\n324: \"Only the eight pinned upstream files were read; the rest of the upstream tree (routing, migrations,\n325: templates, tests) is outside the evidence set, so claims about upstream beyond these files are not\n326: made.\"\n327: \n328: ### 3\n329: \"Restart durability is specified but not demonstrated: the probe cannot stop or start the server, so\n330: the cross-process restart check exists only as an operator procedure in README.md and is not claimed\n331: as executed.\"\n332: \n333: ### 4\n334: \"The probe compares API-level shapes only; it asserts nothing about the HTML surface, so the\n335: accessibility, Post/Redirect/Get and focus requirements of the UI are reviewed by hand rather than\n336: machine-checked.\"\n337: \n338: ### 5\n339: \"Drag-and-drop is deliberately out of P0: the UI exposes movement through buttons and a select, so\n340: pointer-only drag affordances, live drag feedback and cross-column drag previews are not covered.\"\n341: \n342: ### 6\n343: \"The target stores no due dates, recurrence rules, assignees, labels, priorities, attachments,\n344: comments, checklists, activity history or archive, so the leaf's 'card detail' and 'standard\n345: capabilities' layers are absent by design in P0.\"\n346: \n347: ### 7\n348: \"Deletion is destructive without archive or undo, so a mistaken board deletion cannot be recovered\n349: in this P0 build (decision D5).\"\n350: \n351: ### 8\n352: \"The HTTP server is single-threaded and the probe runs sequentially, so concurrent writers, lock\n353: contention and busy_timeout behaviour are not exercised.\"\n354: \n355: ### 9\n356: \"No authentication, TLS, per-board permissions or multi-user isolation: the process must stay on the\n357: loopback interface.\"\n358: \n359: ### 10\n360: \"Column rename has no per-column history and card edits record no change log, so 'the position\n361: confirmed in the card's history' from the leaf is not implemented in P0.\"\n362: \n363: ### 11\n364: \"The upstream MIT licence covers the pinned reference files only; no upstream source was copied into\n365: the target, and a file-level licence audit of the upstream tree was not performed.\""}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/milin/2026/软件开发/application-atlas/docs/template-factory/go-minimax-batch-2026-10-21/personal-kanban/calls/blueprint-spec-review-aux/source/candidate/REVIEW_SCOPE.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1792580628564,"end":1792580628766}}}}
